{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>When you acquire a slave.</b> This pericope is placed here at the beginning because He redeemed them from being slaves and commanded them never to enslave one another, a brother, with rigor, for all generations — only six years. When you acquire: Rashi interpreted it as immediately through a court that sold him for his theft, or alternatively it is only one who sells himself due to poverty; but if a court sold him, he does not go out at six. And if you ask: how can one say that if a court sold him he does not go out at six, when it is written כי ימכר לך אחיך העברי או העבריה ועבדך שש שנים — וכי ימכר (when he is sold) implies through others, which suggests that a court sold him for his theft? Rather, in the Mekhilta it is interpreted thus: כי תקנה (when you acquire) — immediately through a court that sold him for his theft — and on this I say: שש שנים יעבוד — and even the son is subject to him, and all the laws written in this pericope apply to him. And how do I fulfill ועבדך שש שנים? Not for the son and not for a fellow. Alternatively — כי תקנה — it is only one who sells himself because of poverty — and since he sold himself of his own will, he works the son, and all the laws written here apply to him — but if a court sold him — for his theft, sold against his will — the master has no authority to give him a Canaanite maidservant against his will, and he does not work even the son — and he always goes out at six. When it says כי ימוך אחיך עמך וגו׳ — this is a voluntary seller — and by reasoning he works the son, for from where would a limitation come to say that he does not work the son? For the verse ועבדך is written regarding one sold by a court, that he works the son. Here Rashi interpreted: כשהוא אומר כי ימוך אחיך [עמך] ונמכר לך הרי מוכר עצמו וכו׳. And if you ask: the voluntary seller himself, from where do we know that he goes out at six? It is learned from a gezerah shavah of שכיר–שכיר with the mountain: just as the change of a hired man's wage, stated regarding one sold by a court — as it is written כי ימכר לך אחיך העברי או העבריה ועבדך שש שנים — so too שכיר כתושב, stated regarding one who sells himself, goes out at six. שש שנים יעבוד: an allusion to the six kingdoms that enslaved Israel: Egypt, Assyria, Babylon, Media, Greece, Edom. From then onward ובשבעית יצא לחפשי." "<b>He shall serve.</b> In the manner he is accustomed to serve, and not slave labor and rigor, as other verses elsewhere demonstrate." "<b>And in the seventh.</b> At its beginning. Seventh for selling but not for shemittah, for we do not find release for a Hebrew slave except in the Jubilee, and it is written שש שנים יעבוד." "<b>He shall go out free.</b> I have already said: אשר הוצאתיך מארץ מצרים מבית עבדים — they are in My hand and are not permitted to sell themselves, as it is written כי לי בנ״י עבדים וגו׳, and My deed of ownership precedes the deed of others." "<b>Free.</b> Some interpret that the lamed is superfluous, like the lamed in והשלישי לאבשלום — just as there it means והשלישי אבשלום (and the third is Absalom), so too here: יצא חפשי, and the yod in it is a yod of relation, like the yod in קדמוני, שלישי, רביעי, and the like. And some say it is like לחופש, i.e., a noun of freedom, and the yod is superfluous in it like the yod in היושבי, המגביהי, המשפילי, and their companions. If he was ill and recovered — one might think he should repay him for his idleness; therefore it says יצא לחפשי חנם. <b>Without payment.</b> Without a bill of emancipation and without a deduction of money, unlike a Canaanite slave, who requires a bill of emancipation, and unlike the Hebrew maidservant, whose release involves a deduction of money; but this one goes out with nothing — therefore it says חנם."], :book :exodus, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {0 "The comment cites Rashi's interpretation by abbreviation (פרש״י / פירש״י) without variation; the Mekhilta derivation is presented as the compiler's own resolution of the difficulty.", 2 "The point is that שביעית here means the start of the seventh year of servitude, not the sabbatical year of land-rest; the phrase שביעית למכר ולא לשמטה is rendered as a technical distinction.", 4 "The phrases המגביהי / המשפילי are technical grammatical examples of superfluous yod in participle forms; the passage המקראי appears to cite forms from the liturgical poem format."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:59:25.895237675Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}